--- !u!114 &-3367199332703154105
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: NarrativeParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: "<b>Physically based lighting</b> attempts to simulate the correct behavior of light, it is a key requirement to achieve believable lighting results, especially when targetting a photorealistic look. One important factor is the intensity of the lighting: HDRP relies on real-life lighting units, such as Lux and Lumen.\n\nWorking with real-life units and intensties will improve the consistenty of the lighting and facilitate the calibration between different types of lighting, especially between natural and artificial lighting. These intensities can easily be found online (e.g. online shopping and manufacaturers websites). \n\nIn addition, relying on these real-world values greatly reduces the burden on beginners when it comes to selecting the correct light intensities.\n\nHere are some href=\"https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.render-pipelines.high-definition@latest/index.html?subfolder=/manual/Physical-Light-Units.html%23lighting-and-exposure-diagram\"<a>examples</a> of different light sources and their respective lighting values."
--- !u!114 &-1481348611727371926
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: NarrativeParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: "1. Expand the GameObject <b>Spotlight_01a.</b>\n\n2. Select <b>Light_Spot_1600lm,</b> the light illuminating the wall, and inspect its properties.\n\nThe intensity of this lamp is <b>1600 Lumen</b> and the temperature is <b>3500 Kelvin.</b> This corresponds to the intensity of a powerful 100W halogen bulb, with a warm tint. \n\nThe intensity of the light bulbs is usually provided by the manufacturer, in Lumen or Candela.\n\nCreating realistic lighting is as simple as looking up bulb values online, or using the handy sliders and presets in the Light's inspector.\n\n\n<b>Expert tips</b>\n\n\u2022 When using Lumens with a Spot light, use the <b>Reflector</b> mode (enabled by default), in order to have a correct simulation of the light intensity based on the cone angle of the Spot light. If you cannot see this checkbox, \"Show Additional Properties\" via the 3-dots menu in the Emission foldout.\n\n\u2022 To optimize performance, keep the Emission and Shadow <b>Fade Distance</b> properties as low as possible, so that the light and its shadows disappear beyond a certain distance (in meters)."
--- !u!114 &-854350778819091975
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: NarrativeParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: "In this tutorial, you learned how to use Physically Based Light intensities and colors to achieve realistic lighting.\n\nWhen talking about physically correct lighting, it\u2019s crucial to understand Exposure, which we will cover in the next tutorial.\n\nHDRP also supports <a href=\"https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.render-pipelines.high-definition@latest/index.html?subfolder=/manual/IES-Profile.html\">IES profiles</a> which are files that stores the distribution of light around a lamp. For instance, these files are frequently used in architecture visualistions.\n\nFor more information on Physically Based Lighting, watch this <a href=\"https://youtu.be/yqCHiZrgKzs?t=1936\">video tutorial.</a>\n\n<b>Note:</b> You do not need to save your changes in order to follow other tutorials."
